/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Safety & Survival (batch z) */
WB.L("choking-first-aid", "safety", "beginner", "Choking: back blows and chest thrusts",
  "Most people picture the Heimlich manoeuvre. Australian first aid guidelines teach something different, and the reason is a surprising story about evidence.",
  [
    ["A silent emergency", "Severe choking is usually silent, because a fully blocked airway leaves no air to speak, cough or breathe.",
      "Movies make choking loud, but a person with a fully blocked airway usually can't speak, cough or breathe. They may clutch their throat, look panicked, and their lips and face may turn blue or grey. The brain is starved of oxygen within minutes, so the response must be fast.\n\nChoking is a risk at every age. Toddlers explore with their mouths, and grapes, nuts, popcorn and hard lollies are common culprits. Older adults are at risk too, especially when swallowing has weakened after a stroke or with conditions such as dementia. Meat is a classic cause in adults."],
    ["Coughing is good news", "If the person can cough forcefully, encourage them to keep coughing and stay with them.",
      "The simplest test is whether the person can cough effectively. A strong, noisy cough means air is still moving, and coughing is the body's best tool for clearing the airway. Encourage them to keep coughing. Don't slap their back or give them water while they cough well.\n\nStay with them and watch closely, because a partial blockage can become a complete one. If coughing doesn't clear it, call Triple Zero (000). A weak, silent or ineffective cough, difficulty breathing or an inability to speak signals a severe blockage. Then you act and call for help at the same time."],
    ["Back blows first", "For severe choking in a conscious person, call 000 and give up to five sharp back blows, checking after each one.",
      "Under Australian guidelines from the Australian and New Zealand Committee on Resuscitation (ANZCOR), the first step for a conscious adult or child with severe choking is back blows. Call 000, or have someone else call.\n\nStand slightly behind the person, support their chest with one hand and bend them well forward, so gravity helps. With the heel of your other hand, give a sharp blow between the shoulder blades. Check after each blow whether the object has come out, rather than automatically delivering all five. The aim is a sudden jolt that loosens the blockage so it falls forward out of the mouth, not deeper."],
    ["Then chest thrusts", "If back blows fail, give up to five chest thrusts, then keep alternating the two methods.",
      "If five back blows haven't cleared the airway, switch to chest thrusts. Support the person's back with one hand. Place the heel of your other hand on the lower half of the breastbone, the same spot used for CPR, and push in sharply. Chest thrusts are slower and sharper than CPR compressions. Each one squeezes a burst of air out of the lungs, like a cough you create for them.\n\nCheck after each thrust. If the blockage remains after five, go back to five back blows, and keep alternating until the object comes out, help arrives, or the person becomes unresponsive."],
    ["Heimlich and babies", "Australian guidelines don't recommend abdominal thrusts, and babies get head-down back blows and two-finger chest thrusts.",
      "In 1974 the American surgeon Henry Heimlich popularised abdominal thrusts. They are still taught in places such as the United Kingdom and United States, though advice varies. ANZCOR's guideline says abdominal thrusts are not recommended, citing reports of life-threatening complications such as injured organs, and finds back blows and chest thrusts work with low risk. If you live in Australia, learn the local method.\n\nFor babies under one, call 000 and lay the baby face down and head-down along your thigh or forearm, supporting the head and jaw. Give up to five back blows, then turn them over for up to five two-finger chest thrusts, and keep alternating.\n\nIf anyone collapses and stops breathing normally, start CPR."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is severe choking often silent?", ["Most people faint before they can make a sound","No air can pass, so they can't speak or cough","The person is too embarrassed to make a noise","The throat swells shut within a few seconds"], 1, "With no air moving, the person can't shout, cough or breathe, so you must watch for the signs."],
    [1, "If a choking person is coughing forcefully, what should you do?", ["Give them a drink of water to ease the cough","Give sharp back blows straight away","Lay them flat on their back to rest","Encourage them to keep coughing and stay with them"], 3, "A strong cough is the body's best tool for clearing the airway, so let it work and watch closely."],
    [2, "How should you position someone for back blows?", ["Lying flat on their back on the floor","Sitting upright and leaning back in a chair","Leaning well forward so gravity helps","Lying on their side with knees drawn up"], 2, "Bending forward lets gravity help, so the object moves out rather than deeper."],
    [3, "What is the correct sequence if back blows don't work?", ["Abdominal thrusts until the object clears","Up to five chest thrusts, then alternate","More back blows only, as hard as possible","Offer sips of water to wash it down"], 1, "Alternating back blows and chest thrusts continues until the object clears, help arrives or the person collapses."],
    [4, "Why do Australian guidelines not recommend abdominal thrusts?", ["They have been shown never to work at all","They are thought to work only on children","They can cause serious injuries to organs","They need special equipment to be done safely"], 2, "Reports of serious injuries led Australia to favour back blows and chest thrusts, which work with low risk."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most people picture the Heimlich. Australian guidelines teach something else."});

WB.L("severe-bleeding", "safety", "beginner", "Stopping severe bleeding",
  "A person can lose a dangerous amount of blood in minutes. The most powerful tool for stopping it is something everyone already owns: their hands.",
  [
    ["Minutes matter", "Severe bleeding can become life-threatening within minutes, so act while you call 000.",
      "An adult holds roughly five litres of blood. A wound to a major vessel in the arm, leg, neck or groin can bleed heavily enough to become life-threatening within minutes. Paramedics are fast, but not always fast enough, so the person beside the injured one matters most.\n\nBleeding is life-threatening if blood is spurting or pulsing, flowing steadily, pooling on the ground, or soaking through clothing and bandages. First check for danger to yourself. Then call Triple Zero (000), or ask someone else to call while you act. Put the phone on speaker so the operator can coach you."],
    ["Press hard", "Firm, direct pressure on the wound is usually the quickest and most effective way to stop bleeding.",
      "Australian first aid guidelines are clear: direct pressure is usually the quickest and most effective way to stop bleeding. Put a pad, such as folded cloth, gauze or a clean T-shirt, on the wound and press down hard with both hands. If nothing is available, use bare hands. Infection can be treated in hospital, but blood loss cannot be undone.\n\nLean in and use your body weight. Keep the pressure steady rather than lifting to check, because each peek can let a fragile clot break. Wear gloves if you have them, but don't waste time hunting for them."],
    ["Add, don't remove", "If blood soaks through, leave the first pad in place and put another on top.",
      "The instinct is to peel away a blood-soaked dressing and replace it. Resist it. Removing the first pad can tear away the clot forming underneath and restart the bleeding. Place a second pad over the first and bind it firmly with a bandage, or keep pressing.\n\nHelp the person lie down, especially if the bleeding is from a leg or is severe, and keep the injured part still. Lying down reduces the risk of fainting and helps blood reach the brain.\n\nIf an object such as a knife or glass is stuck in the wound, leave it. It may be plugging the hole. Pad around it and press on the pads either side, then call 000."],
    ["Using a tourniquet", "For life-threatening limb bleeding that pressure cannot stop, a trained first aider can use a tourniquet.",
      "A tourniquet is a tight band around an arm or leg, above a wound, that cuts off blood flow to the limb. Old first aid courses taught people to fear them. Military experience, including in Iraq and Afghanistan, showed they save lives in catastrophic limb bleeding.\n\nAustralian guidelines now say to use one for life-threatening limb bleeding that direct pressure cannot control, if one is available and you are trained. Place it a short distance above the wound, not over a joint or the wound itself, following the product's instructions, and tighten until the bleeding stops. Note the time it was applied and tell paramedics. Never loosen it yourself."],
    ["Shock and minor bleeds", "Pale, clammy skin and a fast pulse can signal shock, and most everyday bleeding is minor and easily managed.",
      "Even after bleeding is controlled, the body may struggle. Shock is a dangerous drop in blood flow to the organs. Signs include pale, cold, clammy skin, a rapid weak pulse, fast breathing, anxiety, confusion and drowsiness. Keep the person lying down, reassure them and cover them to stop heat loss. Give no food or drink, since they may need surgery. If they become unresponsive and stop breathing normally, start CPR.\n\nMost bleeding is minor. Rinse small cuts, press with a clean cloth and cover. For a nosebleed, sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for at least ten minutes. Tilting the head back is wrong, as blood runs down the throat."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the person next to an injured person matter most in severe bleeding?", ["Only bystanders are allowed to touch a wound","Ambulances do not attend bleeding injuries","Help may not arrive before the blood loss is critical","Bleeding stops on its own after a few minutes"], 2, "Serious bleeding can become fatal within minutes, so bystander action is what buys time."],
    [1, "What is the quickest and most effective way to control severe bleeding?", ["Washing the wound thoroughly first","Raising the arm above the head and waiting","Applying a bandage loosely to avoid pain","Firm, direct pressure with a pad or hands"], 3, "Pressing hard and steadily controls the bleeding while help is on the way."],
    [2, "What should you do if blood soaks through the first pad?", ["Remove it and put on a fresh pad","Leave it there and add another pad on top","Stop pressing so the wound can breathe","Rinse the pad under running water"], 1, "Removing the pad can tear away the forming clot and restart the bleeding."],
    [3, "When is a tourniquet appropriate under Australian guidelines?", ["For limb bleeding that pressure cannot control","For any cut on an arm or a leg that bleeds","For nosebleeds that last several minutes","For bleeding from the neck or the head"], 0, "A tourniquet is a last resort for life-threatening limb bleeding that pressure alone cannot stop, used by someone trained."],
    [4, "What is the correct way to treat a nosebleed?", ["Tilt the head back and pinch the bridge","Lie flat and wait for it to stop","Push tissue deep into the nostril","Sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part"], 3, "Leaning forward keeps blood from running down the throat, and sustained pinching lets a clot form."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Severe bleeding can turn critical in minutes. Your own hands are the best first tool."});

WB.L("seizure-first-aid", "safety", "beginner", "Seizures: what to do and what not to do",
  "Watching someone have a seizure is frightening, and the most famous piece of advice about it is completely wrong. The right response is calm, simple and mostly about keeping them safe.",
  [
    ["An electrical storm", "A seizure is a sudden burst of abnormal electrical activity in the brain, and it has many possible causes.",
      "The brain runs on electrical signals passed between billions of nerve cells. During a seizure, a sudden burst of abnormal, synchronised activity disrupts that traffic. What happens depends on where it starts and how far it spreads.\n\nEpilepsy is a tendency to have recurring seizures. But seizures can also be triggered by a high fever in young children, a head injury, very low blood sugar, alcohol withdrawal, some drugs, or an illness affecting the brain. Seizures are more common than many people think, and many of us will witness one, so basic seizure first aid is worth knowing."],
    ["Not all look alike", "Some seizures cause convulsions, while others look like staring, confusion or odd repeated movements.",
      "The type most people picture is a tonic-clonic seizure, once called grand mal. The person may cry out, stiffen, fall and jerk rhythmically, sometimes with noisy breathing, drooling or loss of bladder control. Most stop on their own within a few minutes.\n\nMany seizures look nothing like this. In a focal seizure, a person may seem dazed, pluck at clothes, smack their lips, wander or not respond properly. An absence seizure, most common in children, can look like a few seconds of blank staring, easily mistaken for daydreaming. Someone having a quieter seizure may be misjudged as drunk or rude when they actually need patience and protection."],
    ["Time it, protect them", "Note the time, move dangers away, cushion the head and don't restrain the person.",
      "Stay with the person and stay calm. Check the time straight away, because how long the seizure lasts is one of the most important things to know. Move hard or sharp objects away and put something soft, like a folded jacket, under their head. If it's safe, loosen anything tight around the neck.\n\nDon't hold them down or try to stop the movements. Restraint can injure both of you and won't end the seizure. Don't move them unless they are in danger, such as near traffic, water, stairs or fire. Ask onlookers to give them space and privacy."],
    ["The tongue myth", "Never put anything in a person's mouth during a seizure, because it is impossible to swallow your tongue.",
      "For generations, people were told to force something between the teeth of a person having a seizure, to stop them swallowing their tongue. This is a harmful myth. The tongue is anchored to the floor of the mouth and cannot be swallowed.\n\nPushing a spoon, wallet or fingers into the mouth can break teeth, injure the jaw, cause choking on fragments and get your fingers badly bitten. A person may bite their tongue or cheek, which can bleed, but this usually heals and is far less dangerous than what well-meaning bystanders cause.\n\nWhen the movements stop, roll the person onto their side into the recovery position. Give no food or drink until they are fully awake and able to swallow."],
    ["Recovery and calling 000", "Stay with the person as they recover, and call 000 if the seizure is long, repeated, in water or causes injury or breathing trouble.",
      "Confusion, drowsiness or upset can last several minutes or longer after a seizure. Speak calmly, tell them where they are and stay until they're alert. Some people carry an identification card, wear a medical bracelet or have a seizure management plan.\n\nMany people with known epilepsy don't need an ambulance for a typical short seizure. But Epilepsy Action Australia advises calling 000 if it lasts over five minutes, another follows before recovery, or they don't wake up. Call too if they are injured, have trouble breathing, are pregnant or diabetic, it's their first seizure, or you're unsure. Any seizure in water is an emergency. Febrile seizures in young children are usually harmless, but have a doctor check a first one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a seizure?", ["A blockage of blood flow to the brain","A reaction that occurs only in epilepsy","A burst of abnormal electrical activity in the brain","A loss of muscle strength from low blood sugar"], 2, "Seizures come from abnormal brain activity and can have causes other than epilepsy."],
    [1, "Which statement about seizures is correct?", ["All seizures involve falling and jerking","Seizures happen only to people with epilepsy","Some look like staring or confusion, not convulsions","A seizure always lasts longer than ten minutes"], 2, "Absence seizures are easily mistaken for daydreaming, so not every seizure involves convulsions."],
    [2, "What should you do during a convulsive seizure?", ["Hold the person still to stop the movements","Note the time, clear hazards and cushion the head","Move them to a quiet room straight away","Pour cold water over their face"], 1, "Timing the seizure and protecting from injury is the core of first aid, and restraint can cause harm."],
    [3, "Why should you never put anything in a seizing person's mouth?", ["It helps keep the airway open for the person","The seizure will last longer if they bite down","It is only dangerous when the person is a child","Tongues can't be swallowed, and objects cause injury"], 3, "Swallowing the tongue is a myth, and forcing objects in breaks teeth and risks choking."],
    [4, "When should you call 000 for a seizure?", ["Only if the person has not been diagnosed with epilepsy","Only if they bite their tongue during the seizure","If it lasts over five minutes, repeats or is in water","Whenever the person falls asleep once it is over"], 2, "Long, repeated, first-time or in-water seizures carry higher risk and need an ambulance."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"You cannot swallow your tongue. So why was that advice believed for generations?"});

WB.L("electrical-safety", "safety", "beginner", "Electricity: the danger you can't see",
  "Electricity gives no warning: no smell, no sound, nothing to see. A few simple rules, and one small switch in your meter box, stand between ordinary life and a fatal shock.",
  [
    ["Why current kills", "Electric current can stop the heart and cause deep burns that look small from outside.",
      "Current crossing the chest can throw the heart into a chaotic rhythm called ventricular fibrillation, where it quivers instead of pumping. That is cardiac arrest. Current can also make muscles contract so strongly a person can't let go of what they're holding. And as it passes through tissue, it generates heat and burns along its path.\n\nEntry and exit wounds can look small while muscles, nerves and blood vessels inside are badly damaged. Anyone who has had a significant shock or electrical burn should be checked by a doctor, even if they feel fine."],
    ["Safety switches", "A safety switch (RCD) cuts power in a fraction of a second when current leaks, and it's one of the home's great lifesavers.",
      "A residual current device, called a safety switch in Australia, constantly compares the current going out along the active wire with the current returning along the neutral. If they don't match, current is leaking somewhere, possibly through a person. The switch cuts the power within a fraction of a second, fast enough to greatly reduce the risk of death.\n\nSafety switches differ from circuit breakers and fuses, which mainly protect wiring from overload. Newer homes must have them, but many older homes have them on only some circuits, or none. Most have a test button worth pressing regularly."],
    ["Leave it to licensed", "Electrical wiring work in Australia must be done by a licensed electrician, and simple habits prevent most home hazards.",
      "Across Australia, it's illegal for an unlicensed person to do electrical wiring work, such as installing power points, replacing light switches or running cables. Faulty wiring can kill, sometimes years later and someone else entirely, and a loose connection can start a fire inside a wall.\n\nAt home, the habits are simple. Don't use appliances with frayed or damaged cords. Don't overload power boards or daisy-chain them. Keep appliances away from water and never touch switches with wet hands. Never fish toast from a plugged-in toaster with a knife. If a switch or power point feels warm or smells of burning, get it checked."],
    ["Powerlines down", "Treat every fallen powerline as live and stay at least eight metres away, even if you're in a car.",
      "After storms, crashes and bushfires, powerlines can lie on the ground, fences, cars or trees. Treat every fallen line as live, even if it isn't sparking, because it may be re-energised automatically. Distributors advise staying at least eight metres away, about two car lengths. Electricity can travel through wet ground, puddles, fences and metal, so you needn't touch the line to be hurt. Call 000 if someone is injured or there's a fire.\n\nIf a line falls on your car, stay inside unless it's on fire. If you must leave, jump clear with your feet together, never touching the car and ground at the same time, then shuffle away with small steps."],
    ["Helping, and looking up", "Never touch someone in contact with electricity, switch off the power first, and look up before raising anything tall.",
      "If someone is being electrocuted, don't grab them. The current can flow into you too. Switch off the power at the switchboard or wall, or unplug the appliance if safe. For powerlines and high voltage, don't approach: call 000 and wait for the electricity company. Once power is off, check them using DRSABCD, start CPR if needed, send for a defibrillator and cool burns under cool running water for twenty minutes.\n\nMany serious accidents happen outdoors when tall objects meet overhead lines: ladders, tipper trays, boat masts. Electricity can also arc across a gap. Look up before raising anything tall, carry long objects horizontally, and never climb poles, towers or train roofs."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why should anyone with a significant electric shock see a doctor even if they feel fine?", ["Electric shocks always cause infections","The heart always stops after a shock","Doctors must check every shock by law","Skin wounds can hide serious internal damage"], 3, "The damage inside muscles, nerves and vessels can be far worse than the burn on the skin suggests."],
    [1, "How does a safety switch protect you?", ["It stops wiring overheating under load","It cuts the power fast when current leaks","It lowers your household power bill","It stores power for use in emergencies"], 1, "By spotting a mismatch between outgoing and returning current, it shuts off power before a shock becomes fatal."],
    [2, "Who may legally carry out electrical wiring work in Australia?", ["Any adult following a manual","A homeowner doing a small job","Only a licensed electrician","Anyone supervised by a friend"], 2, "Faulty wiring can kill or start a fire years later, which is why the work is restricted."],
    [3, "What should you do if a powerline lands on your car?", ["Get out and run to the nearest building","Open the door and hold the metal frame","Drive away as quickly as possible","Stay inside unless it's on fire, and call 000"], 3, "The safest place is usually inside, unless fire forces you out."],
    [4, "What is the first step when helping someone being electrocuted?", ["Switch off the power before touching them","Pull them clear with your bare hands","Splash water on them to cool them","Wait for them to let go on their own"], 0, "Touching them while they're still in contact with power can make you a second casualty."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"No smell, no sound, nothing to see. What stands between you and a fatal shock?"});

WB.L("poisons-and-button-batteries", "safety", "beginner", "Poisons at home: one number to remember",
  "Most poisonings happen not in labs or crime novels but in ordinary kitchens, bathrooms and handbags. Knowing one phone number, and one danger hiding in your remote control, could save a child's life.",
  [
    ["Accidents at home", "Most poisonings are ordinary accidents at home, and children under five are the most at risk.",
      "Poisoning sounds like something from a detective story, but most cases are home accidents. Children under five are especially vulnerable because they explore with their mouths, copy adults and move fast. A child can climb onto a bench, open a handbag or find a dropped tablet in seconds.\n\nCommon culprits include medicines, cleaning products, dishwasher and laundry pods, essential oils, cosmetics, garden chemicals and some plants. Adults are poisoned too, often through medication mix-ups like a double dose, or chemicals kept in the wrong containers. A few habits prevent most of it."],
    ["13 11 26", "Save 13 11 26, the Poisons Information Centre, and call 000 first if the person is collapsed, seizing, struggling to breathe or very drowsy.",
      "Australia's national poisoning advice number is 13 11 26, the Poisons Information Centre. It's answered around the clock, every day, from anywhere. Specialists can tell you whether a substance and amount is likely to be harmful, what to do now and whether to go to hospital. Many calls end with reassurance. Save the number in your phone and put it on the fridge.\n\nBut if the person has collapsed, is having a seizure, is having trouble breathing or is very drowsy, call Triple Zero (000) first.\n\nDon't make them vomit, or give milk, unless the Poisons line or a doctor says so. Vomiting can burn corrosives twice or draw petroleum products into the lungs. Rinse skin and eyes with running water. Keep the container."],
    ["Button batteries", "A swallowed button battery can burn through tissue within two hours, so go straight to hospital.",
      "Small coin-shaped lithium batteries are in car keys, remote controls, kitchen scales, thermometers, toys and musical greeting cards. If a child swallows one and it lodges in the oesophagus, it reacts with saliva and body fluids to produce a strongly alkaline chemical that can burn through tissue, sometimes within two hours. The damage can reach major blood vessels. Children have died in Australia this way.\n\nSymptoms may be vague or absent at first, so don't wait for them. If you suspect a child has swallowed a button battery, or put one in their nose or ear, call 000 or go straight to a hospital emergency department, and call 13 11 26 for advice."],
    ["Laws born of grief", "After children died, Australia introduced mandatory button battery standards in 2022, but older products and spare batteries remain a risk.",
      "In 2013, four-year-old Summer Steer of Queensland died after swallowing a button battery no one knew she had found. Her death and others led to campaigns by families, doctors and consumer groups. In 2022, Australia introduced mandatory safety standards, enforced by the ACCC. Products with button batteries must have secure compartments young children can't easily open, batteries must be in child-resistant packaging, and warnings are required. Australia was among the first countries to introduce mandatory rules like this.\n\nStill, older products, cheap imports and spare batteries in drawers remain a risk. Store them up high and dispose of used ones safely."],
    ["Storage and wild poisons", "Safe storage prevents most poisonings, and wild mushrooms should never be eaten unless an expert has identified them.",
      "Keep medicines, including vitamins and pain relievers, in a locked or child-resistant cupboard at least 1.5 metres off the ground, not in handbags or on bedside tables. Put them away straight after use, even while answering the door. Never store chemicals in drink bottles. Keep products in original labelled containers. Ask visitors to keep bags out of reach. Give medicines with the lights on, reading the label.\n\nSome of the most dangerous poisons are natural. The death cap mushroom, introduced to Australia, grows around Canberra and Melbourne, often under oaks. Cooking doesn't destroy its toxins, and part of one can cause liver failure, with symptoms delayed for many hours. Never eat wild mushrooms unless an expert is certain. After eating an unknown mushroom or plant, call 13 11 26 at once and keep a sample."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are children under five most at risk of poisoning?", ["They are given more medicines than adults","They explore with their mouths and move quickly","They are naturally resistant to bitter tastes","They spend more time outdoors than adults"], 1, "Curiosity, mimicry and speed combine to make small children especially vulnerable."],
    [1, "When should you call 000 instead of 13 11 26?", ["When you are unsure what the product was","When a child has only touched the product","When the person has collapsed or is seizing","When the label says 'keep out of reach'"], 2, "Emergencies come first. The Poisons line is for advice when the person is still alert."],
    [2, "Why are swallowed button batteries so dangerous?", ["They explode as soon as they are swallowed","They release heavy metals that cause slow illness","They always cause immediate, obvious choking","They react with body fluids and burn tissue quickly"], 3, "The chemical burn can start quickly, so act without waiting for symptoms."],
    [3, "What problem remains despite Australia's 2022 battery standards?", ["Older products and spare batteries are still a risk","Batteries can no longer be sold in shops","Battery compartments are now too hard for adults","Warnings are no longer required on products"], 0, "Standards cover new products, so loose and older batteries still need safe storage."],
    [4, "What is the safest rule about wild mushrooms?", ["Cook them thoroughly for several minutes first","Never eat one unless an expert has identified it","Eat only a small amount the first time you try","Avoid only the ones growing under oak trees"], 1, "Death cap toxins survive cooking, and symptoms can be delayed for hours."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The most dangerous thing in your remote control isn't what you'd expect."});

WB.L("carbon-monoxide", "safety", "intermediate", "Carbon monoxide: the invisible killer",
  "It has no colour, no smell and no taste, and its symptoms feel like the flu. Here is how an ordinary heater can quietly poison a home, and how to stop it.",
  [
    ["Two brothers", "Two young brothers died in 2010 from a faulty gas heater, with no smoke, smell or alarm to warn them.",
      "In May 2010, two brothers, Chase and Tyler Robinson, died in their sleep at their rented home in Shepparton, Victoria. The cause was carbon monoxide from a faulty gas heater. Nothing had seemed wrong: no smoke, no strange smell, and no alarm, because the home had no carbon monoxide detector.\n\nA coronial inquest examined who was responsible for maintaining the heater. Their mother, Vanessa Robinson, became a prominent campaigner for gas heater safety, and the case helped drive change, including regular gas safety checks in Victorian rental homes. It is a painful reminder that the most dangerous hazards may be ones we cannot sense."],
    ["How it harms", "Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin far more strongly than oxygen does, pushing oxygen out of your blood.",
      "Carbon monoxide is made when fuels such as gas, wood, charcoal, petrol or diesel burn without enough oxygen. Red blood cells carry oxygen using haemoglobin. Carbon monoxide binds to it roughly two hundred times more strongly than oxygen does, so even small amounts steadily push oxygen out of the blood. The brain and heart suffer first.\n\nThe gas is colourless, odourless and tasteless, so people breathe it unaware. High levels can cause unconsciousness and death within minutes. Lower levels over hours or days can cause lasting brain and heart damage."],
    ["Symptoms that fool", "Early signs look like flu, so clues such as several people feeling sick at home are what give it away.",
      "Early symptoms are ordinary: headache, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness, weakness, breathlessness and confusion. People assume flu, migraine or a stomach bug. Clues point to carbon monoxide instead. Several people, or pets, feel sick at once. Symptoms are worse at home or with a heater running, and improve outside.\n\nPeople who are asleep or have been drinking may lose consciousness before noticing, so night-time poisoning is especially dangerous. Babies, older people, pregnant women and people with heart or lung disease are more vulnerable."],
    ["Sources and servicing", "Faulty gas heaters and indoor barbecues or generators are common sources, so service heaters with a licensed gasfitter.",
      "A major source in Australian homes is gas space heaters, especially older or poorly maintained ones. An open-flued heater should send fumes out through a flue, but a blocked or faulty flue, or exhaust fans pulling air the wrong way, can spill carbon monoxide inside. Unflued heaters release fumes into the room and need good ventilation. Other causes are barbecues, camping stoves, patio heaters or charcoal used indoors, in tents, caravans or garages, generators near open windows, and engines running in closed garages.\n\nHave gas heaters serviced by a licensed gasfitter, generally at least every two years or as the manufacturer recommends. Warning signs include a yellow or orange flame, soot or stains, and a stuffy room. If you rent, ask when the heater was last checked."],
    ["Alarms and action", "Smoke alarms can't detect carbon monoxide, so fit a separate alarm, and if you suspect poisoning, get everyone out and call 000.",
      "Smoke alarms don't detect carbon monoxide. A separate alarm from a hardware store warns you when levels rise. Place it per the instructions, typically near fuel-burning appliances and sleeping areas, and test it regularly. Requirements for them vary by state and territory, but regulators encourage them, especially with gas heating. An alarm is a backstop, not a substitute for servicing.\n\nIf you suspect poisoning, get everyone, including pets, into fresh air. If quick and safe, switch off the appliance and open doors on your way out. Call 000. Start CPR if someone isn't breathing normally. Don't return until a licensed gasfitter has checked the source. Treatment is high-concentration oxygen, and everyone should be assessed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made the Shepparton heater so dangerous?", ["It produced thick smoke that filled the house","It caused a fire that spread silently overnight","The gas gives no smoke, smell or taste to warn you","It released a strong gas smell that was ignored"], 2, "The home had no detector, and the gas itself is undetectable by the senses."],
    [1, "How does carbon monoxide harm the body?", ["It burns the delicate lining of the lungs","It binds to haemoglobin and pushes oxygen out","It thickens the blood so it cannot flow properly","It paralyses the muscles used for breathing"], 1, "It binds far more strongly than oxygen, starving the brain and heart first."],
    [2, "Which clue most suggests carbon monoxide rather than flu?", ["Symptoms appear only after eating a meal","Only one person in the house has a headache","A high fever develops over a day or two","Several people and pets feel worse at home"], 3, "Group symptoms that ease away from home point to a shared environmental cause."],
    [3, "How often should gas heaters generally be serviced by a licensed gasfitter?", ["Only when they stop heating the room properly","Every ten years, before the coldest winter","At least every two years, or as the maker says","Only before they are used for the first time"], 2, "Regular servicing and a carbon monoxide test catch leaks before they harm anyone."],
    [4, "Why can't a smoke alarm protect you from carbon monoxide?", ["It only works during daylight hours","Smoke alarms don't detect the gas at all","It detects the gas but sounds too quietly","It is switched off while a heater is running"], 1, "A dedicated carbon monoxide alarm is the only warning device, and it supplements regular servicing."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"It has no colour, smell or taste, and it feels like the flu. Meet the invisible danger."});

WB.L("crocodile-safety", "safety", "intermediate", "Croc country: staying safe in the north",
  "Northern Australia is home to the largest reptile on Earth, an ambush hunter that has been perfecting its technique for millions of years. The rules for living alongside it are simple, and they work.",
  [
    ["The saltwater crocodile", "Saltwater crocodiles are the largest living reptile and ambush hunters across northern Australia.",
      "The estuarine or saltwater crocodile, Crocodylus porosus, is the largest living reptile. Large males can grow beyond five metres and weigh many hundreds of kilograms, while females are considerably smaller. In Australia they live across the tropical north, from the Kimberley through the Northern Territory to Queensland, down the east coast into central Queensland.\n\nThey are ambush predators. A crocodile can lie almost submerged with only its eyes and nostrils showing, wait a long time for an animal to come to the water's edge, then lunge with astonishing speed. Australia's other species, the freshwater crocodile, is smaller and far less dangerous, though it can still bite."],
    ["A remarkable comeback", "Protection brought crocodiles back strongly, so more big animals now share waterways with people.",
      "After the Second World War, saltwater crocodiles were hunted intensively for their skins, and populations crashed. Western Australia, the Northern Territory and Queensland introduced protection between about 1970 and 1974. Numbers have since bounced back strongly, especially in the Northern Territory, where the wild population is estimated at around one hundred thousand.\n\nIt's one of conservation's great success stories, but it has a consequence. More and larger crocodiles now share rivers, beaches and boat ramps with growing numbers of residents and tourists. Fatal attacks remain rare in Australia, a few at most each year, and nearly all involve people in or right beside the water."],
    ["Assume they're there", "Salties live well beyond the sea, so assume any northern water may hold a crocodile, sign or not.",
      "The name is misleading. Salties live in tidal rivers, mangroves and estuaries, but also in freshwater billabongs, swamps, waterholes and rivers far from the coast. They swim along the coast and can turn up on popular beaches and around islands. A clear, inviting waterhole with no sign may still hold one.\n\nCrocodiles are hard to spot. Muddy water, reflections and camouflage mean even locals can miss a large animal a few metres away. They're regularly seen in waterways near Darwin, Cairns and Townsville. Wildlife authorities give one rule: in croc country, assume any water may contain crocodiles, and don't swim unless you are certain it's safe."],
    ["Be CrocWise", "Keep well back from the water, don't leave food or scraps near it, and don't repeat routines at the water's edge.",
      "CrocWise guidance and similar advice from the Northern Territory and Western Australia dramatically reduces risk. Obey warning signs, but never assume water without signs is safe. Don't swim, paddle or wade where crocodiles may live. Stand a few metres back when fishing, and never on overhanging logs. Don't clean fish or leave scraps, bait or food near the water, boat ramps or campsites. Never feed crocodiles. Be extra alert at night and during the roughly September to April breeding season.\n\nCamp at least fifty metres from the water and two metres above the high water mark. Collect water in a bucket from a distance. Crocodiles learn routines, so vary your spot. In boats, keep limbs inside. Canoes and kayaks aren't recommended."],
    ["Myths and reporting", "Zigzag running is a myth; the real risk is the lunge from the water, so keep your distance and report sightings.",
      "The popular idea is to run in a zigzag because crocodiles can't turn quickly. Experts regard this as a myth. Crocodiles are fast over short distances but rarely chase people away from water. The real risk is the sudden lunge at someone on the bank, wading or swimming. Keep your distance in the first place.\n\nEach state and territory with crocodiles has a way to report sightings, which helps officers decide whether one needs to be removed. Report crocodiles that act aggressively, approach people or boats, or turn up in towns. If someone is attacked, call 000. Don't enter the water yourself. Once they're out, control bleeding with firm direct pressure, keep them warm and lying down, and follow DRSABCD."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do saltwater crocodiles typically hunt?", ["Chasing prey over long distances on land","Hunting in coordinated packs in open water","Lurking almost submerged, then lunging at the bank","Stalking prey on open ground around midday"], 2, "Their ambush technique makes the water's edge the danger zone."],
    [1, "What is a consequence of the saltwater crocodile's recovery?", ["Fatal attacks now happen across the north every week","They are now found only in remote wilderness areas","They have disappeared from most northern rivers","More large crocodiles now share waterways with people"], 3, "Protection worked, so people and crocodiles now meet more often at rivers, beaches and boat ramps."],
    [2, "What is the best rule for water in croc country?", ["Swim only if there are no warning signs","Assume any water may hold a crocodile, sign or not","Swim only in water with a clear bottom","Swim only in freshwater, never in salt water"], 1, "Salties live in fresh and salt water, and are hard to see, so signs alone aren't enough."],
    [3, "How far from the water should campers set up, according to CrocWise?", ["At least fifty metres back and two metres above high water","Within about ten metres, for easy access to water","Right on the sloping bank for a good view of the water","Anywhere, as long as there are no warning signs nearby"], 0, "Staying back reduces the chance of a crocodile learning your routines or ambushing you."],
    [4, "What do experts say about the zigzag running advice?", ["It is the best way to escape a chasing crocodile","It works only against freshwater crocodiles","It works only when running on soft ground","It is a myth, as the real risk is the lunge"], 3, "Crocodiles rarely chase people away from water, so prevention beats escape tactics."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"It's the largest reptile on Earth, and it's not only found where you'd think."});

WB.L("cold-water-hypothermia", "safety", "intermediate", "Cold water and hypothermia",
  "Many people who die in cold water drown before hypothermia ever sets in. The first minute is the most dangerous, and the instinct to swim hard can be exactly the wrong one.",
  [
    ["More than a polar risk", "Hypothermia is an Australian risk too, and water pulls heat from the body far faster than air.",
      "Hypothermia isn't only for polar explorers. In Australia, people get into trouble with cold regularly: bushwalkers in Tasmania caught by sudden storms, even in summer, skiers and hikers in the Alps, rock fishers swept into winter seas, and kayakers and boaters who capsize in cold rivers and lakes.\n\nWater is the great accelerator, drawing heat from the body far faster than air at the same temperature. Wind and wet clothing do something similar on land. Add rain, tiredness and inadequate clothing, and a person can become dangerously cold on a seemingly mild day, especially in the mountains, where weather can change within an hour."],
    ["Cold water shock", "Falling into cold water triggers an involuntary gasp and rapid breathing that can cause drowning within the first minute.",
      "When a person suddenly falls into cold water, the shock on the skin triggers an involuntary gasp, followed by rapid, uncontrollable breathing, a racing heart and a spike in blood pressure. This cold shock response peaks in the first minute or so.\n\nIf your head is underwater during that first gasp, you can inhale water and drown almost immediately, long before the body cools. The racing heart can also trigger heart problems in people at risk. That is why many cold water deaths happen close to shore or a boat, and why even strong swimmers can drown quickly after an unexpected fall."],
    ["Float first", "After falling in, float on your back until the cold shock passes, rather than swimming hard.",
      "The instinct is to thrash and swim hard. But swimming and panicking during cold shock makes breathing harder to control and raises the risk of inhaling water. Water safety groups, including Britain's Royal National Lifeboat Institution, now teach: float first.\n\nLean back, spread your arms and legs, and keep your face out of the water, moving your hands gently if needed, until the gasping and fast breathing settle, typically within a minute or two. Then you can think clearly: signal for help, grab something that floats, or swim to safety if it's close. A lifejacket makes all of this far easier.\n\nThe 1-10-1 rule of thumb sums up the timing: roughly one minute to control your breathing, ten minutes of meaningful movement, and an hour or more before unconsciousness."],
    ["Signs and first aid", "Hypothermia is a core temperature below about 35 degrees, and the person needs shelter, dry layers, gentle handling and a 000 call.",
      "Hypothermia means core temperature has dropped below about 35 degrees Celsius, from a normal of around 37. Early signs are intense shivering, cold pale skin and fumbling hands. Then come the 'umbles': stumbling, mumbling, fumbling and grumbling, with slurred speech and slow, confused thinking. In severe cases shivering stops, a dangerous sign rather than a good one. Watch each other closely, because judgement fades early.\n\nCall 000 for anything beyond mild hypothermia. Move them out of wind, rain or water and handle them gently. Replace wet clothes with dry layers or wrap them in blankets, including head and neck, and insulate from the ground. If alert, give warm sweet drinks, never alcohol. Don't rub limbs or use a hot bath. Start CPR if they stop breathing normally."],
    ["Reach or throw", "Don't enter the water to rescue someone: call 000 and reach or throw them something that floats.",
      "Each year people drown trying to rescue others, often family members, including children and dogs. The safest approach is reach or throw, don't go.\n\nCall 000. Then reach the person from a safe position with a pole, branch, rope or towel, lying down on the bank for balance, or throw something that floats, such as a lifebuoy, an esky, a bodyboard or a ball. Tell them to float on their back and keep calm. Only trained rescuers with proper equipment should enter the water, especially if it's cold or fast-flowing.\n\nPrevention is simpler: wear a lifejacket when boating or rock fishing, dress in layers, check the forecast and tell someone where you're going."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does cold water chill the body so much faster than air?", ["It is always colder than the air around it","It stops the body from shivering properly","It draws heat from the body far faster than air","It reacts with the natural oils on the skin"], 2, "Water pulls heat out quickly, so cold water is dangerous even on a mild day."],
    [1, "What makes cold water shock so deadly in the first minute?", ["The body freezes solid within seconds","An involuntary gasp can let you inhale water","Blood stops flowing to the brain at once","The muscles lock up the instant you land"], 1, "The gasp reflex can cause drowning long before the body cools."],
    [2, "What should you do first after falling into cold water?", ["Swim hard straight towards the nearest shore","Take off your heavy clothes straight away","Tread water with your head tilted forward","Float on your back until your breathing settles"], 3, "Floating lets the cold shock pass so you can think and act clearly."],
    [3, "Why is it a dangerous sign when a hypothermic person stops shivering?", ["It can mean hypothermia has become severe","It means the person is starting to warm up","It shows the cold shock has now passed","It means they should be given cold drinks"], 0, "Shivering stopping can signal that the body is losing its ability to cope with the cold."],
    [4, "How should you help someone in trouble in cold water?", ["Swim out and carry them back to shore","Jump in with a rope tied around yourself","Call 000, then reach or throw them a float","Wait for them to swim to shore themselves"], 2, "Reach or throw, don't go, because rescuers often drown too."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Many cold water deaths come before the body cools. The first minute is the danger."});

WB.L("crash-scene-first-aid", "safety", "intermediate", "First on the scene of a car crash",
  "One day you may be the first person to arrive at a crash. What you do in the first few minutes, starting with protecting yourself, can decide how many people go home.",
  [
    ["Protect yourself first", "Make yourself safe before approaching a crash, because the scene is often dangerous for rescuers.",
      "The instinct is to rush to the wreckage. But traffic is still moving, drivers are distracted, and visibility may be poor. Rescuers are sometimes struck by other vehicles. Start by making yourself safe. Slow down and park well clear of the crash, ideally off the road. Turn on your hazard lights. If it's dark, wear something bright or use a torch, and set up a reflective triangle well back if you have one. Keep children and passengers in your car or well away from the road. Then assess the scene before approaching."],
    ["Call 000 well", "Call 000 early and give your location first, then describe the crash and stay on the line.",
      "Call Triple Zero (000) as early as possible, or ask a bystander to call while you check on people. The most important information is where you are. Look for road names, cross streets, kilometre markers, landmarks or direction of travel. The free Emergency+ app, developed by Australia's emergency services, shows your GPS coordinates to read out.\n\nThen describe the crash: how many vehicles, how many injured, whether anyone is trapped, unconscious or bleeding badly, and whether there's fuel leaking, smoke or fire. Stay on the line; the operator can guide you through first aid."],
    ["Check the hazards", "Look for fuel, fire, powerlines and unstable cars before approaching, and leave high-voltage parts to the crews.",
      "Before approaching, look for dangers. Is there spilt fuel, smoke or fire? Fallen powerlines, which could make the vehicle and ground dangerous? Is the car unstable, on its side or on a slope? Is traffic still flowing?\n\nIf it's safe, ask occupants to turn off the ignition, or do it yourself if you can without risk, and make sure nobody smokes nearby. Don't approach a vehicle with powerlines on it; stay at least eight metres away and warn others. Electric and hybrid vehicles can carry high-voltage components, so leave anything beyond basic first aid to emergency crews. If you can't make it safe, stay back and wait."],
    ["Moving the injured", "Leave injured people where they are unless there's immediate danger, but keep the airway open for anyone unresponsive.",
      "People in a crash may have hidden neck or spine injuries, and movement could make them worse. As a rule, leave injured people where they are and keep them still. Support their head if they're conscious and it's comfortable. Reassure them that help is coming and ask them not to move their head. The exception is immediate danger: fire, rising water or traffic. Then getting them out matters more. Keep head, neck and body aligned as best you can.\n\nIf someone is unresponsive, the airway is often the biggest threat. Australian guidelines say keeping it open outranks the possibility of spinal injury. Follow DRSABCD. If they're breathing, support the airway and roll them onto their side if needed. If not, they need CPR."],
    ["Helmets and duty", "Leave a motorcyclist's helmet on unless it blocks airway management, and remember that drivers involved must stop and help.",
      "Should you remove a motorcyclist's helmet? Generally, leave it on. It may support a neck injury, and incorrect removal could cause harm. If the rider is conscious and breathing, open the visor, talk to them and keep them still. The helmet only comes off if it stops you managing their airway or breathing, for example with vomiting or CPR. Ideally two people do it: one supports the head and neck, the other gently spreads the helmet and eases it off.\n\nAustralian road rules require drivers in a crash to stop, help anyone injured and exchange details. Witnesses are not usually obliged to stay, but their account helps. It's normal to feel shaken afterwards. A first aid course is the best preparation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why should you park well clear of a crash and turn on hazard lights?", ["It stops fuel leaking from the crashed cars","It warns approaching drivers and protects rescuers","It switches off the crashed cars' engines","It makes the scene easier to photograph"], 1, "Protecting yourself first means you can help without becoming another casualty."],
    [1, "What is the most important information to give the 000 operator?", ["The make of the cars","Who caused the crash","Exactly where you are","The number plates"], 2, "Responders can't help until they know where to go."],
    [2, "Why should you stay well back from a vehicle with a powerline on it?", ["Its doors lock to trap anyone close by","The fuel inside will certainly ignite","The line's weight could crush the vehicle","The car and the ground near it may be live"], 3, "Fallen lines can energise the vehicle and the ground around it, so stay at least eight metres away."],
    [3, "When should you move an injured person from a crash?", ["As soon as you reach them and check them","When fire or traffic puts them in danger","Whenever they say they feel able to sit up","Only once the police have arrived on scene"], 1, "Escaping an immediate threat outweighs the risk from a possible spinal injury."],
    [4, "What is the general advice for an injured motorcyclist's helmet?", ["Always take it off straight away","Cut it off as the very first step","Leave it on unless it blocks their airway","Remove it only after the ambulance arrives"], 2, "The helmet may support a neck injury, so remove it only when airway care requires it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The first minutes at a car crash may depend on a step most people skip."});

WB.L("disaster-psychology", "safety", "advanced", "How people really behave in disasters",
  "We imagine stampedes and mass panic. Decades of research tell a stranger story: in a crisis, the real danger is often that people stay calm, slow and polite for far too long.",
  [
    ["The panic myth", "Mass panic is rare in disasters; people mostly stay calm and help each other.",
      "Films show crowds screaming and trampling one another. Real disasters rarely look like that. From the 1950s, sociologists such as Enrico Quarantelli studied fires, floods, earthquakes and bombings and found that true mass panic, meaning wild, selfish flight, is uncommon. Most people stay orderly. Strangers help strangers, families stick together, and survivors are usually the first rescuers, pulling neighbours from rubble long before professionals arrive.\n\nFatal crowd crushes do happen, but investigators usually blame overcrowding and poor crowd management, not people losing their minds. The myth does harm too, because it can make authorities reluctant to share frightening information quickly."],
    ["Delay and milling", "The real danger in a crisis is delay: we explain warnings away, then wait for others to act first.",
      "If panic is not the problem, delay is. When an alarm sounds or smoke appears, we first assume a drill, a false alarm or burnt toast. This is normalcy bias. It usually serves us well, because most alarms are false, but in a real emergency those minutes matter. People in burning buildings have finished meals and waited for lifts.\n\nEven once we accept something is wrong, we seek information first: phoning family, asking colleagues, scanning social media. Researchers call this milling. If everyone waits for everyone else, each person reads the calm around them as proof of safety. Studies of the World Trade Center evacuation on 11 September 2001 found many survivors delayed leaving to make calls or gather belongings."],
    ["When people freeze", "Under extreme stress some people freeze, and clear, loud, simple instructions often get them moving.",
      "A third response is freezing. Under sudden, overwhelming stress, some people become stuck, unable to decide or move. This is not cowardice. It reflects the brain struggling to process an unfamiliar situation quickly enough. Survivors of plane crashes and shipwrecks describe fellow passengers sitting motionless as fire or water approached.\n\nThe British psychologist John Leach, who studied survival behaviour, has suggested that only a minority of people respond quickly and effectively in a sudden crisis, most are bewildered for a time, and a small minority behave in counterproductive ways. The exact proportions are debated, but the pattern is widely recognised. The practical lesson is that people who freeze often respond well to clear, loud, simple instructions from someone else."],
    ["Practice saves lives", "Rehearsal and following instructions save lives, because under stress the brain falls back on what it has practised.",
      "Rick Rescorla, a former soldier, headed security for Morgan Stanley at the World Trade Center. After the 1993 bombing he was sure the towers would be attacked again, so he made staff practise stairwell evacuations despite their grumbling. On 11 September 2001 he ignored advice to stay put and ordered them out of the South Tower. Almost all of the firm's roughly 2,700 staff there survived. He went back inside to help others and was killed when the tower collapsed.\n\nAircraft are designed to be emptied in about 90 seconds, but only if people carry nothing. On 2 January 2024 at Tokyo's Haneda Airport, all 379 people escaped a burning Japan Airlines A350, widely credited to crew training and passengers leaving their bags behind."],
    ["Train your future self", "Know your exits, decide what you will do in advance, and act early on any warning.",
      "A few habits help. When you enter a hotel, theatre, plane or new workplace, notice the nearest exits and count the rows or doors to them, because smoke makes it hard to see. Decide in advance, while calm: if a fire alarm sounds, you leave immediately; on a severe bushfire day, you leave early rather than wait and see. Practise household fire escape plans.\n\nTreat any warning as real until proven otherwise. An unnecessary evacuation costs little, while a late one can cost everything. Your phone and bag can be replaced. And in a crisis, be the person who says, clearly and calmly, we need to go now."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What have disaster researchers found about mass panic?", ["It is the main cause of deaths in disasters","It is rare, and most people stay calm and help others","It happens in nearly every fire, but only briefly","It is common in crowds but rare among families"], 1, "Studies of real disasters show calm, cooperative behaviour is far more common than wild, selfish flight."],
    [1, "Why can milling be dangerous in an emergency?", ["It makes people run in circles and trample each other","Everyone waits on others, and calm looks like safety","It sends crowds towards the nearest exit all at once","It spreads rumours that can set off a stampede"], 1, "When no one moves, each person takes the group's calm as evidence that there is no danger."],
    [2, "What often helps a person who has frozen during a crisis?", ["Being left alone to calm down","Waiting quietly until they decide for themselves","Clear, loud, simple instructions from someone else","A detailed explanation of what is happening"], 2, "Freezing reflects a brain struggling to process the situation, so short, direct instructions can break it."],
    [3, "Why did regular evacuation drills help Morgan Stanley's staff on 11 September 2001?", ["People under stress fall back on what they have practised","Drills made the stairwells wider and safer","Drills taught staff to wait for official advice","Practice removes all fear in an emergency"], 0, "Rehearsal turns a confusing emergency into a routine the brain can follow."],
    [4, "What is the best response when a fire alarm sounds in a building?", ["Check social media first to confirm it is real","Finish what you are doing, then leave if it continues","Treat it as real and leave at once","Wait for others nearby to start moving"], 2, "An unnecessary evacuation costs little, while a late one can cost everything."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Panic is rare in disasters. The real danger is something much quieter."});

